  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does veto mean?",
      "a": "Veto is Latin for I forbid. A leader with a veto can refuse to approve a law. In the USA, Congress can override a presidential veto with a two-thirds vote in both houses.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a presidential veto?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is impeachment?",
      "a": "Impeachment is when the legislature formally accuses a top official, like a president, of serious wrongdoing. If found guilty in the trial that follows, they can be removed. Brazil, South Korea and the USA have all used it.",
      "asks": [
        "How can a president be removed?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Can a court cancel a law?",
      "a": "In many countries, yes. Top courts such as India's Supreme Court or Germany's Constitutional Court can strike down laws that break the constitution. This is called judicial review.",
      "asks": [
        "What is judicial review?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a vote of no confidence?",
      "a": "In a parliamentary system, members vote on whether they still support the government. If most say no, the prime minister usually resigns or calls an election.",
      "asks": [
        "How is a prime minister removed?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who checks the government apart from the branches?",
      "a": "Voters, who can change leaders at elections, and a free press, which reports mistakes. Many countries also have auditors who check spending and ombudsmen who handle complaints.",
      "asks": [
        "What else keeps leaders honest?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "India's Supreme Court set out the basic structure doctrine in the Kesavananda Bharati case in 1973.",
    "A US presidential veto can be overridden by a two-thirds vote in both the House and the Senate."
